<p>A site and a channel are two doors into the same house. Everything here is built from the system’s own tokens — the same accent, the same Geist, the same hand, the same patterns, the same motion — so a viewer who arrives from a video finds the place they expected, and a reader who clicks through to the channel does too.</p>

<h2>What is here</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong><a href="/content-creation/youtube/">YouTube</a></strong> — channel art drawn like a page with a cover across it, profile art, the end screen, and the Pixelmator templates that rebuild any frame as editable layers.</li>
	<li><strong><a href="/content-creation/youtube/thumbnails/">Thumbnails</a></strong> — ten styles of one system: film, vlog, travel, Q&amp;A, series, live, commentary, skit, tutorial, short — and the grid they are judged in, four a row.</li>
	<li><strong><a href="/content-creation/elements/">Elements</a></strong> — what a video is cut from: moving backgrounds, media frames, the logo sting, twenty ways in and sixteen ways out, loops, titles, lower thirds, HUD callouts, reusable scenes, the skeleton, colour grades, and the two cuts that are the system’s own.</li>
	<li><strong><a href="/content-creation/live/">Live stream</a></strong> — the sources (a tally light, a ticker, a camera frame, chat, alerts, a goal) and the four scenes assembled from them.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How a frame works</h2>
<p>Every frame is a page under <code>/frames/</code> at its real size — a thumbnail is 1280 × 720, a scene 1920 × 1080, channel art 2560 × 1440 — with <code>im-studio</code> on the root and <strong>one unit inside: the em</strong>, set from the frame’s shorter side (27 of them), so the same markup is right at every size. It is a web page, so it takes a real photograph, live text and the real fonts, and it is never out of date with the site.</p>
<div class="im-table-scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Use it as</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A picture</td><td><code>npm run frames</code> exports every frame as a PNG at its exact size through your own Chrome. Or open the frame at 100% and screenshot it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Editable layers</td><td><code>npm run frames -- --layers</code> then <code>npm run pixelmator</code> rebuilds each frame in Pixelmator Pro as the picture plus live text layers in the real fonts — see <a href="/content-creation/youtube/pixelmator/">Pixelmator templates</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A browser source</td><td>OBS, Streamlabs, vMix: add the frame’s URL at 1920 × 1080. Overlays have no ground of their own.</td></tr>
<tr><td>In an edit</td><td>The frames are plain HTML on <code>im.css</code>; any tool that renders a web page — including an HTML-first video renderer — can put one on the timeline. Every layer has an <code>in</code>, a time and an <code>out</code>, so a frame is already a clip. Motion tokens are published as <code>assets/im-motion.json</code>.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>The rules a frame keeps</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>One accent, on one thing.</strong> A word in the title, the live dot, a chip — never a fill behind the words. (The skit is the one exception, and that is why it reads as a skit.)</li>
	<li><strong>The person is the picture.</strong> A portrait fills the right half and fades into the ground; a channel is a person, and a face is what a thumb stops on.</li>
	<li><strong>Less text, more type.</strong> A kicker and a title of five or six words, set huge, one word in the accent. Nothing under the title.</li>
	<li><strong>One word in the hand.</strong> Caveat, tilted, the accent: “say hi!”, “day 12”, “wait, what?”. It is the one thing on the frame that is not typeset, which is what makes it read as a person.</li>
	<li><strong>One thing per style.</strong> Each thumbnail changes one thing from the film. The rest stays, so a channel of mixed films reads as one channel.</li>
	<li><strong>The mark signs every frame</strong>, small, bottom left: the S and the dot.</li>
	<li><strong>The dot means on air.</strong> It is the cut between scenes, the lamp on the camera, the full stop after the name. Nowhere else.</li>
</ul>
